  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Chances for rain Sunday. Precipitation chances increasing \nfor Wednesday through Thanksgiving.\n\nDry weather will continue through Saturday, with increasing cloud \ncover by the beginning of the weekend, as a coastal low organizes \nand begins to track up the Atlantic Seaboard over the weekend. The \nlow initially slow moves northward, then picks up speed as it gets \nabsorbed into the broader 500mb trough reaching the eastern US. \nThere is still considerable uncertainty in the track of this low, as \nguidance continues to vary among models and from run to run. \nHowever, there is decent confidence that areas east of the mountains \nwill see rain, but how much is where the uncertainty lies. \nAmounts have trended slightly higher across the Piedmont, \nbetween a quarter to half an inch from the Blue Ridge and east. \nThere is a 10% chance of 2.5\" or greater in the Piedmont, and \neven up to 2\" as far west as the I-81 corridor. The low end \namount, or 90% chance of exceedance, is 0.0\" areawide. That all \nbeing said, there is considerable spread in model guidance for \nrainfall amounts this weekend, and is heavily dependent on the \nexact track of the low. Any rain in this region would be \nbeneficial, given the persistent Severe to Extreme drought \nconditions across southern VA and eastern NC.\n\nThe upper trough will bring a cold front through the area late in \nthe weekend as well, with strong high pressure following closely \nbehind. Low rainfall chances for the mountains come with this \nfrontal passage, with the better upper level support farther to\nthe north. The high pressure will bring dry and brisk \nconditions for the first half of next week. A cold air damming \npattern builds over the Appalachians as the surface high tracks \nacross the northeast towards the middle of the week, and another\npotential system develops near the southeast. This is forecast \nto track into the Mid Atlantic near Thanksgiving, which could \nbring another round of precipitation to the area. With enough \ncold air in place over the region, could see a mix of rain and \nsnow in parts of the area. While specific impacts to the local \narea are uncertain at this time, but this is the next system to\nwatch, especially as the timing looks to coincide with major \ntravel days. But, overall, the possibility for a chilly, \nsomewhat windy, and wet Thanksgiving is increasing.",
